A surface is not stale before it has ever been heard from

A native client's next paint is granted by compositor-proxy's own clock:
`wl_surface.frame` is intercepted and answered from `FrameFeedback`, never
forwarded to the browser. Before queueing a callback, `commitNotify` asks
whether the browser is still alive by comparing the last encoder-feedback
message against a 1500 ms window, and a surface that fails goes on
`parkedFeedbackClockQueue`.

THAT QUEUE HAS NO TIMER. The clock tick walks `feedbackClockQueue` only, so a
park lasts until the next feedback message arrives -- and the browser sends
those from `setInterval(feedbackLoop, 1000)`, and only for a surface that has
already completed a decode.

Two things follow, and both were measured rather than reasoned.

1. `clientFeedbackTimestamp` starts at **0**, which reads as "last heard from at
   the epoch". So every surface fails the test on its FIRST commit, however
   healthy the browser is. Measured with one window, a proxy on loopback and a
   browser on the same machine:

       PARKSTATS parked=1 ran=33 worstStaleMs=2031

   One park, at the start, on a pipeline that was never late. A menu is a new
   surface every time it opens, so a menu paints once and then sits still --
   photographed.

2. 1500 ms against a 1000 ms sender is one and a half sending periods of
   margin. A main thread that slips half a second -- which a guest doing
   software H.264 decode of a full-screen window does routinely -- trips the
   test while the browser is working perfectly, and pays a full parked second.

The parking itself is kept: it is real backpressure for a browser that has gone
away. It is the two thresholds that were wrong.

--- a/src/FrameFeedback.ts
+++ b/src/FrameFeedback.ts
@@ constructor
     parkedFeedbackClockQueue: ... = []
-    clientFeedbackTimestamp = 0
+    // Not 0: that reads as "last heard from at the epoch" and parks the first
+    // commit of every surface before the browser has had any chance to report.
+    clientFeedbackTimestamp = performance.now()
     frameCallbackDelay = 0
@@ commitNotify
-        const clockQueue = performance.now() - this.clientFeedbackTimestamp > 1500
+        // Five missed messages is a browser that has gone away; one and a half
+        // is jitter. The sender's period is 1000 ms (EncoderFeedback.js).
+        const clockQueue = performance.now() - this.clientFeedbackTimestamp > 5000
             ? this.parkedFeedbackClockQueue
             : feedbackClockQueue

---

And a third, on the same clock: a countdown that subtracts zero never finishes.

The tick subtracts `tickInterval` from each pending callback's
`frameCallbackDelay` and fires it at <= 0. The period is programmed from the
browser -- `nextTickInterval = Math.floor(clientRefreshInterval)` -- and
`refreshInterval` is sampled in EncoderFeedback.js from a rAF chain that starts
at 0 and only re-samples on a change of more than 16 ms:

    if (Math.abs(newRefreshInterval - refreshInterval) > 16) refreshInterval = newRefreshInterval

A page whose frame deltas are consistently under 16 ms -- a display faster than
about 62 Hz, or any run of quick frames -- never lifts `refreshInterval` off 0.
`tickInterval` is then 0, every tick subtracts 0, and once `frameCallbackDelay`
has grown past zero (it is `max(encode, decode)`, so it does as soon as anything
has been measured) NO FRAME CALLBACK IS EVER DELIVERED AGAIN.

Not observed on this host, which samples ~16.7 ms and lands the other side of the
threshold. It is one comparison away and the failure is total.

@@ updateDelay
-        nextTickInterval = Math.floor(clientRefreshInterval)
+        nextTickInterval = Math.max(1, Math.floor(clientRefreshInterval))
